/// The root file declaring names its own import exports.
///
/// ⚠️ Legal, and both interpreters have always run it: the nearest binding wins,
/// which is the same rule that lets a declaration shadow a built-in. It did not
/// COMPILE. The duplicate-export check refuses two modules sharing a name, and
/// exempted the root on the grounds that the root's symbols are `static` and so
/// cannot collide. `static` does not fail to collide — it collides differently:
///
///     error: static declaration of 'v_Twin' follows non-static declaration
///
/// So the root was the one file left where a compiler emitted a program it could
/// not build, inside the exemption written to say that was safe.
///
/// ⚠️ The qualified lines are the second half and are easy to lose. The root's
/// colliding symbol is renamed to keep it distinct, and a rename that also
/// applied to `Twin.Twin` would silently return the root's own value — breaking
/// the one form whose whole purpose is to reach past the shadow. It did, in both
/// emitters, until the rename was suspended for qualified names.
///
/// ⚠️ The unit is called `Pair` and not `Twin` on purpose. A file-scope `var`
/// outranks a unit of the same name, so a root that shadows `Twin` also shadows
/// a *unit* called `Twin` and cannot qualify against it — correct, documented,
/// and not what this program is for.

uses 'lib/Pair';
uses 'lib/Outer';

var Twin := 'root-var';

function Twain () : String;
begin
    Exit 'root-fn';
end

class Twinned;
begin
    function Hello () : String;
    begin
        Exit 'root-class';
    end
end

type Shade = (Pale, Deep);

begin
    // The root's own declarations win, unqualified.
    WriteLn (Twin);
    WriteLn (Twain ());
    WriteLn (Twinned().Hello());

    // ...and the unit's are still reachable through it.
    WriteLn (Pair.Twin);
    WriteLn (Pair.Twain ());
    WriteLn (Pair.Twinned().Hello());

    // -- an enum, whose members are a third symbol kind in the header ------
    WriteLn (Pale);
    WriteLn (Shade.Deep);

    // ⚠️ Identity, not spelling: an enum member is interned by its type, so the
    // root's Pale and the unit's are different objects however alike they read.
    WriteLn (Pale = Shade.Pale);
    WriteLn (Pale = Pair.UnitShade ());

    // -- the same collision one file over ----------------------------------
    //
    // lib/Outer declares PRIVATE names colliding with what lib/Inner, its own
    // import, exports. Nothing outside Outer can name a private, which is the
    // same condition that lets the root rename its own -- so the two are one
    // rule rather than two.
    WriteLn (Mine ());
    WriteLn (Theirs ());
end
